WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.958
MAX SALTONSTALL: On this
episode of Stack Chat,

00:00:01.958 --> 00:00:03.480
Spotify engineer
Matt Brown tells us

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.900
how Kubernetes helps power
the teams that power Spotify.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.880
Increased deployments
and higher productivity

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:09.930
are just the start.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.698
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:14.430
Thanks for joining me, Matt.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.040
So how does Spotify work?

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.120
MATT BROWN: When you use
Spotify on your laptop,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.930
your mobile phone, a
speaker in your house,

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.000
your app is connected
to a layer of services

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.630
that we call the perimeter--

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.387
a series of gateways, load
balancers, things like that.

00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:29.970
And then from there,
depending on what

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.090
you're doing in the app, you're
connected to one of hundreds

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.700
or probably about 1,000
different microservices that

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.660
are developed.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.750
We used to run these
all in our data center.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.220
But over the years, we've
moved fully to Google Cloud.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.490
And those microservices, which
are all talking to each other,

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.460
are developed by about 200
different teams within Spotify.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.050
And they're deploying
those, building

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.768
those independently based on--

00:00:51.768 --> 00:00:54.060
one team might work on the
Search feature, the Playlist

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.430
feature, just different
features like that.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.140
And then we have
another group of teams--

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.880
we have infrastructure
teams where we're

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.470
trying to build
tools for those teams

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.110
to move faster or do things
in a consistent way, things

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:04.840
like that.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.298
MAX SALTONSTALL:
So how do you help

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:09.210
those many, many engineering
teams be able to focus on, I'm

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.670
going to build this
feature and then ship it,

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.635
whether to staging
or production,

00:01:13.635 --> 00:01:15.760
and not have to worry about
all the infrastructure?

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.927
MATT BROWN: So we do a lot
as an infrastructure team

00:01:17.927 --> 00:01:20.190
to try to make it very
simple to stand up

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.740
a new service, a new feature.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.950
There is a framework
that you can

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.770
use that basically gives you
all the basics of building

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.770
a microservice,
handling RPC calls,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.590
logging, connecting
to service discovery

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.163
to find the other services
you need to talk to.

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:38.330
We also make it very simple.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.340
There's a single button you can
press in our developer console

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.430
to just create a new project,
new source code repo.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.810
And you get all that stuff that
I mentioned before basically

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:49.530
for free.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:50.950
You just code it
from a template.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.635
So you're never really
starting from scratch.

00:01:53.635 --> 00:01:56.010
MAX SALTONSTALL: And then you
have to manage all of these

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.930
and figure out how
they're going to actually

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.625
stay alive or scale.

00:01:59.625 --> 00:02:01.680
And you built your own
system to do all that.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:02.680
MATT BROWN: Yes, we did.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:07.560
So we started to adopt Docker,
Docker containers, very early

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.039
on, I think before it
was even version 1.0.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.080
And we were really
excited by it because it

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.210
added a bunch of new things
that we didn't really

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.490
have great answers for
at Spotify at the time.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.820
It used to be very painful
for teams to deploy changes

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.500
to their services.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.690
They'd frequently fail.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.270
Rolling back from a failed
deployment was pretty tricky.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.950
And as a result,
people, teams just

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.590
would not deploy very often
because there was a lot of risk

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:32.640
and pain with that.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.760
So we were very
interested in Docker

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.100
because we thought that
building containers would

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.550
make it a lot simpler.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:40.842
We could just have--

00:02:40.842 --> 00:02:42.300
deployments would
be a lot simpler.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.800
And if you just want to roll
back, because each container is

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.890
kind of atomic and
totally separated,

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.623
that would be very simple.

00:02:48.623 --> 00:02:50.790
Another big benefit is that
it was just a lot easier

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.860
for developers to run
the service themselves

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.600
on their laptop, even
if it didn't really

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.830
look like production.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.980
So the main reason we were
super interested in Docker

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.950
to start with was just to
make deploying easier, making

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:03.540
running services
easier, and then

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.520
to increase the
rate at which people

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.393
were deploying changes and just
being productive, basically.

00:03:08.393 --> 00:03:10.560
MAX SALTONSTALL: And is
there anything, looking back

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.960
on that, at least the first
chunk of that transition,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.377
that you wish you
did differently?

00:03:14.377 --> 00:03:15.752
MATT BROWN: I
would say one thing

00:03:15.752 --> 00:03:17.910
that I wish we did a
little bit differently--

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.120
we were very conscious
as an infrastructure team

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.120
that we're changing out
basically the platform

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.700
that we run applications
on in production, which

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.010
was working pretty fine before.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.500
It had some features that were
missing that we wish we had.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.940
But things weren't on fire
or anything like that.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.523
So we wanted to be
very sure that what

00:03:35.523 --> 00:03:37.387
we move people to
is super stable,

00:03:37.387 --> 00:03:38.970
they don't really
notice any problems,

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:41.940
and it's just all
positives for them to move.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.030
I think as a part of that, we
probably were a little hesitant

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.410
to take risks.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.530
And we didn't want, really,
any team to move their service

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.530
fully to Kubernetes until we
were, as an infrastructure

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:55.890
team, 110% prepared for that.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.680
In retrospect, I think we could
have accepted some more risk.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.480
The engineers on all of our
teams building these services,

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.920
they know better than we do as
an infrastructure team which

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.630
services are super
important, which

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.480
services are internal facing
and not important at all

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.755
and can stand some downtime.

00:04:10.755 --> 00:04:13.380
So think we could have let them
take some more risk and tell us

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:14.772
what would be--

00:04:14.772 --> 00:04:16.480
like do some more
experiments themselves.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.310
And we probably would
have just learned

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:18.750
a few things a
little bit faster.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:19.560
MAX SALTONSTALL: Are
there other things

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.149
that you wish you
did more of as you

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:23.220
were going through this
transition process?

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.340
MATT BROWN: I think one thing
that we found that surprised us

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.510
as we did this is we didn't
have to do as much selling

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:35.700
or evangelizing of the move to
GKE to the engineering teams

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.070
at Spotify.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.400
Part of that's because they
already knew about Kubernetes.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.130
Obviously, it's talked about
quite a lot in the development

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.000
community.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.720
But also, we found that some
early-adopting teams were

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.890
really happy with the results.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.890
And so they would go out
and talk to other teams

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:58.200
and give talks at an internal
conference that we do or just--

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.250
they would try to
motivate their peers.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.520
So I wish one thing we could
have done more of perhaps

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:03.895
is try to take
advantage of that,

00:05:03.895 --> 00:05:07.590
create more
champions internally.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.280
Because those engineers who
did that work were really

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.920
the best selling point that we
had for convincing other teams

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.633
to spend time on this.

00:05:15.633 --> 00:05:17.550
MAX SALTONSTALL: If you
enjoyed this episodes,

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.510
check out the Stack Chat
playlist for more videos.

